  {
   "tipo": "explicacion",
   "titulo": "Verbs that go with «être»",
   "texto": [
    "A few verbs, almost all of them of movement, form the past with **être** instead of «avoir»: **Je suis allé** (I went, I went).",
    "The most important: **aller** (go) → allé, **venir** (come) → venu, **arriver** (arrive) → arrive, **partir** (go) → parti, **rester** (stay) → resté, **sortir** (leave) → sorti, **entrer** (enter) → entered.",
    "All reflexive verbs also go with «être»: **Je me suis levé** (I got up)."
   ]
  },
  {
   "tipo": "explicacion",
   "titulo": "Conformity",
   "texto": [
    "With «être», the participle matches the subject, as an adjective. If a woman speaks, **Je suis allée**. In plural: **Nous sommes allés**; if all are women: **Nous sommes allées**.",
    "You can hardly tell when you talk, but you have to write it down."
   ]
